  view_file {
    absolute_path_uri: "file:///skills/system_skills/image_generation/SKILL.md"
    end_line: 101
    content: "---\nname: \"image-generation\"\ndescription: >\n  Use when the application needs to generate or update images \342\200\224 hero banners, illustrations, icons, backgrounds,\n  avatars, product photos, or any visual asset. Covers image selection strategy\n  (using tools vs assets vs placeholders) and runtime generation using Gemini\n  models.\n---\n\n# Images\n\n## Selection Strategy\n\nChoose images that reinforce the app\'s purpose and visual identity.\n\n-   **Important Assets (Hero images, etc.):** Use the `generate_image` tool to\n    create these, especially if the request is specific. Aim for ~5 generated\n    images per app or less. Use standard aspect ratios (16:9, 1:1, etc.) as\n    detailed below in the prompt guidelines.\n-   **Parallel Batching (Mandatory):** When an app requires visual assets,\n    **audit all visual slots across the entire layout upfront** (hero, Bento\n    cards, avatars, showcases) and **emit all `generate_image` tool calls in\n    parallel within a single turn**. Never split image generation across\n    multiple turns (e.g., generating 2 images first and then adding a 3rd image\n    in the next turn). Because generated filenames contain timestamps, follow\n    this two-phase flow whenever using `generate_image` (even for a single\n    image):\n    1.  *Batch Generation Turn*: Call all `generate_image` tools simultaneously\n        in one turn without writing component files.\n    2.  *Implementation Turn*: Once the tools return with the exact timestamped\n        file paths (e.g. `/src/assets/images/hero_banner_1742081234567.jpg`),\n        write or update the components referencing those exact returned paths.\n        If any tool call fails, use a styled CSS fallback container or SVG icon;\n        never guess filenames.\n-   **Prefer known assets:** Attempt to use official, high-quality, publicly\n    accessible CDN URLs or well-known asset repositories for the rest of the\n    images.\n-   **Fallback:** If `generate_image` is unavailable or fails, use styled CSS\n    fallback containers (e.g. subtle gradient mesh with domain SVG icon and\n    title) or inline SVG. Avoid unverified external hosts (`picsum.photos`,\n    `images.unsplash.com`) as they fail or get blocked in hermetic sandboxes.\n\n## Implementation\n\nAlways use `<img>` elements with the following requirements:\n\n-   **`referrerPolicy=\"no-referrer\"`**: Required on every `<img>` tag to prevent\n    broken images from referrer-restricted hosts.\n-   **Generated Image Paths & `server.ts`**: `generate_image` saves files to\n    `/src/assets/images/<name>_<timestamp>.jpg`. Reference this path as a URL\n    string (`src=\"/src/assets/images/...\"`) or import it only in client-side\n    components\342\200\224never import image files in `server.ts` or shared modules\n    imported by `server.ts` (`tsx` crashes on `.jpg` imports).\n\n```tsx\n<img\n  src=\"/src/assets/images/hero_banner_1742081234567.jpg\"\n  alt=\"Hero banner description\"\n  referrerPolicy=\"no-referrer\"\n/>\n```\n\n## Image Configuration\n\n-   `aspectRatio`: Changes the aspect ratio of the generated image. Supported\n    values are \"1:1\", \"3:4\", \"4:3\", \"9:16\", and \"16:9\", \"1:4\", \"1:8\",\n    \"4:1\",\"8:1\". The default is \"1:1\".\n\n### Aspect Ratio Selection\n\nChoose aspect ratio based on where the image will be used:\n\nUse Case           | Aspect Ratio\n------------------ | ------------\nHero / banner      | 16:9\nAvatar / profile   | 1:1\nCard thumbnail     | 4:3\nMobile full-screen | 9:16\nPortrait           | 3:4\n\n## Edit Images\n\n-   To edit images using the tool, you can prompt with text, images or a\n    combination of both.\n\n## Prompt Guidelines\n\nWrite detailed, descriptive prompts for better image quality:\n\n-   **Be specific**: \"A serene mountain landscape at golden hour with\n    snow-capped peaks reflected in a still alpine lake, photorealistic\" \342\200\224 not\n    \"mountain\".\n-   **Include style**: photorealistic, watercolor, flat design, 3D render,\n    minimalist, isometric, line art, pixel art, etc.\n-   **Specify composition**: close-up, wide-angle, bird\'s-eye view, centered,\n    rule-of-thirds, negative space.\n-   **Match the app\'s theme**: if the app uses a dark theme with neon accents,\n    describe that. If it\'s playful and colorful, reflect that in the prompt.\n-   **Context-appropriate**: hero banners should be expansive landscapes or\n    abstract patterns; product cards should be focused on a single subject;\n    avatars should be portraits or icons.\n"
    num_lines: 102
    num_bytes: 4393
    is_skill_file: true
