  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Request**\n\nI\'m currently dissecting the user\'s new request. I\'ve begun by meticulously examining the provided requirements. The primary objective, as I understand it, involves inspecting the product database and catalog, particularly focusing on active products. I\'m paying close attention to filtering criteria, such as \'active\' status and available stock quantities. This will give me a better understanding of what the user is looking for.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Image Sources**\n\nI\'m now determining how to find the product images. My main concern is to automate the process, so I\'ve established an order of priority for image sources. The goal is to obtain images for the 6 active products, supporting all 10 in the admin workflow. Gemini image generation is only a last resort. Importantly, any generated image must be labeled internally to avoid misrepresentation. First, I will look at the existing products in the database.\n\n\n**Identifying Active Products**\n\nI\'ve narrowed down the scope by filtering for active, in-stock products. I\'ve now identified six specific items that fit the criteria. These include hair accessories like the Rose Gold Pearl Hair Claw and Mulberry Silk Scrunchie Trio, and jewelry such as the Rose Gold Pearl Drop Huggies and the Blush Quartz Layered Necklace. I\'m focusing on these for the image retrieval process.\n\n\n**Evaluating Image Strategy**\n\nI\'m now refining the image strategy. I\'ve noted the existing SVG vector illustrations for the active products and the draft gift hampers. My priority is high-resolution, AI-generated images for the six active products since this is a custom Gurugram brand where original photos are unavailable. I\'ll utilize the `generate_image` tool, with a Gemini fallback if needed, for each product.\n\n\n**Implementing Image Generation**\n\nI\'m now implementing the parallel image generation, aiming for six high-resolution JPGs. Following the strategy, I\'ll call `generate_image` simultaneously for `prod-pearl-claw`, `prod-silk-scrunchie-trio`, `prod-satin-bow-barrette`, `prod-pearl-drop-huggies`, `prod-charm-bracelet`, and `prod-quartz-necklace`. The files will be saved directly in `/src/assets/images/`, and I am actively checking proper storage and labeling.\n\n\n**Refining Image Metadata**\n\nI\'m now focusing on image metadata and admin workflow. My priority is to accurately reflect image origins. For AI-generated images, I\'ll preserve existing color, material, shape, and component information. Crucially, I must avoid inventing non-existent details. I\'ll include \"AI-generated\" metadata, and all AI-generated images will await admin approval. The workflow must be clear, with a status for each product clearly visible on the dashboard.\n\n\n**Integrating Approval Process**\n\nI\'m now carefully integrating the image approval workflow. Building on the previous parallel generation of images for the six active products, I will allow for batch approval of multiple verified images to improve efficiency. I am verifying that the workflow will not publish unapproved or uncertain images. This includes the implementation of a clear admin dashboard display for each product\'s image status. I am also checking against step 6 to confirm correct display in all the specified areas of the site.\n\n\n**Finalizing Image Integration**\n\nI\'m now putting the finishing touches on the image integration. I\'ve focused on ensuring the `product_image_audits` store comprehensive metadata, including image origin, AI model used, and license information. The automated pipeline is in place, prioritizing official sources, then permitted repositories, and finally, Gemini image generation as a fallback, ensuring AI-generated images are clearly labeled. I\'ve also implemented the required admin controls and statuses in `AdminDashboard.tsx` for complete image management.\n\n\n**Defining Admin Workflow**\n\nI\'m now outlining the admin workflow, including all supporting buttons and statuses, and the new `/api/admin/product-images/find-all-available` endpoint. The goal is a highly intuitive, batch-approval process. I am focused on the per-product actions, making sure previews are available with clear metadata. Status display and behavior across all situations is essential.\n\n\n**Confirming End-to-End Test**\n\nI\'m now setting up a comprehensive end-to-end test in headless Chromium to confirm the image approval workflow and ensure all six active products properly render approved JPGs across all specified areas of the site, as documented in Step 6. This includes thorough verification of page refreshes and that existing SVG artwork is correctly rendered. I\'ll focus on database accuracy, double-checking the product information, to ensure prompt generation uses only real product details.\n\n\n**Reviewing Product Descriptions**\n\nI\'m now carefully reviewing the product descriptions and variants to support image generation prompts. I\'m focusing on key details like materials (e.g., \"rose-gold alloy,\" \"mulberry silk\") and identifying specific colors and shapes to ensure accurate image prompts for the AI, especially for the active products. The plan is to use this data for a new image iteration.\n\n\n"
