    user_requests: "Here is the full initial file tree for the application:\n/.env.example\n/.gitignore\n/index.html\n/metadata.json\n/package.json\n/src/App.tsx\n/src/index.css\n/src/main.tsx\n/tsconfig.json\n/vite.config.ts\n\nThe following dependencies are installed:\n@google/genai: ^2.4.0\n@tailwindcss/vite: ^4.3.3\n@vitejs/plugin-react: ^6.1.1\ndotenv: ^17.2.3\nexpress: ^4.21.2\nlucide-react: ^0.546.0\nmotion: ^12.23.24\nreact: ^19.0.1\nreact-dom: ^19.0.1\nvite: ^8.3.0\n\nThe following devDependencies are installed:\n@types/express: ^4.17.21\n@types/node: ^22.14.0\n@types/react: ^19.3.0\n@types/react-dom: ^19.3.0\nautoprefixer: ^10.4.21\nesbuild: ^0.25.0\ntailwindcss: ^4.3.3\ntsx: ^4.21.0\ntypescript: ^7.0.2\n\nKey file contents:\n\n--- /.env.example ---\n```\n# GEMINI_API_KEY: Required for Gemini AI API calls.\n# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime from user secrets.\n# Users configure this via the Secrets panel in the AI Studio UI.\nGEMINI_API_KEY=\"MY_GEMINI_API_KEY\"\n\n# APP_URL: The URL where this applet is hosted.\n# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime with the Cloud Run service URL.\n# Used for self-referential links, OAuth callbacks, and API endpoints.\nAPP_URL=\"MY_APP_URL\"\n\n```\n\n--- /index.html ---\n```html\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <title>My Google AI Studio App</title>\n    <meta name=\"description\" content=\"An application built with Google AI Studio.\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"My Google AI Studio App\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:description\" content=\"An application built with Google AI Studio.\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"website\" />\n    <meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\" />\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div id=\"root\"></div>\n    <script type=\"module\" src=\"/src/main.tsx\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n\n\n```\n\n--- /metadata.json ---\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"\",\n  \"description\": \"\",\n  \"requestFramePermissions\": [],\n  \"majorCapabilities\": [\"MAJOR_CAPABILITY_SERVER_SIDE_GEMINI_API\"]\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /src/App.tsx ---\n```typescript\n/**\n * @license\n * SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n */\n\nexport default function App() {\n  return <div></div>;\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /src/index.css ---\n```css\n@import \"tailwindcss\";\n\n```\n\n--- /src/main.tsx ---\n```typescript\nimport {createRoot} from \'react-dom/client\';\nimport App from \'./App.tsx\';\nimport \'./index.css\';\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(\'root\')!).render(<App />);\n\n```\n\n--- /tsconfig.json ---\n```json\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"target\": \"ES2022\",\n    \"experimentalDecorators\": true,\n    \"useDefineForClassFields\": false,\n    \"module\": \"ESNext\",\n    \"types\": [\"vite/client\"],\n    \"lib\": [\n      \"ES2022\",\n      \"DOM\",\n      \"DOM.Iterable\"\n    ],\n    \"skipLibCheck\": true,\n    \"moduleResolution\": \"bundler\",\n    \"isolatedModules\": true,\n    \"moduleDetection\": \"force\",\n    \"allowJs\": true,\n    \"jsx\": \"react-jsx\",\n    \"paths\": {\n      \"@/*\": [\n        \"./*\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"allowImportingTsExtensions\": true,\n    \"noEmit\": true\n  },\n  \"exclude\": [\"dist\"]\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /vite.config.ts ---\n```typescript\nimport tailwindcss from \'@tailwindcss/vite\';\nimport react from \'@vitejs/plugin-react\';\nimport path from \'path\';\nimport {defineConfig} from \'vite\';\n\nexport default defineConfig(() => {\n  return {\n    plugins: [react(), tailwindcss()],\n    resolve: {\n      alias: {\n        \'@\': path.resolve(__dirname, \'.\'),\n      },\n    },\n    server: {\n      // HMR is disabled in AI Studio via DISABLE_HMR env var.\n      // Do not modify\342\200\224file watching is disabled to prevent flickering during agent edits.\n      hmr: process.env.DISABLE_HMR !== \'true\',\n      // Disable file watching when DISABLE_HMR is true to save CPU during agent edits.\n      watch: process.env.DISABLE_HMR === \'true\' ? null : {},\n    },\n  };\n});\n\n```"
    user_requests: "# PRETTY CHARM \342\200\224 BUILD THE COMPLETE E-COMMERCE PLATFORM FROM ZERO TO WORKING PRODUCT\n\nYou are my senior full-stack engineer, software architect, UI implementation specialist, database engineer, QA engineer and deployment assistant.\n\nYour mission is to build a complete, functional, secure and deployable e-commerce platform named **Pretty Charm**.\n\nThis is not a request for a UI mockup, landing page, static prototype or collection of disconnected components. Build a working application with a customer storefront, persistent database, secure administration, inventory management, order processing, shipment tracking and business reports.\n\nWork in the current project workspace. If it contains an existing project, inspect and reuse it. If the workspace is empty, create the application from scratch.\n\nDo not ask me to implement individual code changes manually. Make reasonable technical decisions, implement the work, run checks, fix errors and continue through the phases. Ask me only when a genuine external decision, secret, legal eligibility issue or business fact is required.\n\n# 1. MANDATORY WORKING RULES\n\n1. Inspect the workspace, available tools, project files and environment before making changes.\n2. Use the uploaded Pretty Charm UI collage as the mandatory visual reference.\n3. Preserve the reference\'s layout, colors, component proportions, typography hierarchy, navigation placement and screen structure as closely as possible.\n4. Do not redesign the interface or replace existing working screens without permission.\n5. Never overwrite or delete unrelated files or existing user data without a safe migration or backup.\n6. Reuse existing frameworks, components, database and authentication where practical.\n7. Do not generate a second application inside the project.\n8. Build real functionality, not decorative buttons or simulated backend operations.\n9. Never fabricate product inventory, reviews, customer orders, payment success, courier scans or delivery estimates.\n10. Do not claim a feature works until it has been tested.\n11. Keep API keys, database credentials and private tokens on the server.\n12. Use maintained dependencies and avoid unnecessary paid services.\n13. Prefer free or low-cost services, but explain any unavoidable hosting, domain, messaging, payment or shipping costs.\n14. Keep all customer-facing prices in INR and use Indian address conventions.\n15. Continue through all implementation phases without requiring approval after every phase. Stop only for genuine blockers that require my credentials, legal eligibility or a real business decision.\n\n# 2. BUSINESS AND BRAND\n\nBrand: Pretty Charm\n\nBusiness type: Online store for gift hampers, hair accessories, jewellery and future beauty/festive products.\n\nShipping origin: Gurugram, Haryana, India.\n\nPrimary market: India.\n\nVisual identity:\n- Premium feminine aesthetic.\n- Blush pink, cream and rose-gold palette.\n- Elegant product photography.\n- Refined typography and subtle shadows.\n- Clean, touch-friendly mobile layout.\n- Responsive design, especially for 360px, 375px, 390px and 430px screens.\n\nUse the existing logo and brand assets. If assets are missing, create clean placeholders that can be replaced later. Do not invent official certifications, testimonials or business credentials.\n\n# 3. REQUIRED CUSTOMER SCREENS\n\nImplement the following screens in the visual style of the uploaded reference:\n\n1. Splash screen.\n2. Sign-up/login screen.\n3. Home page.\n4. Shop/categories.\n5. Product listing and filtering.\n6. Product details.\n7. Wishlist.\n8. Cart.\n9. Checkout and address entry.\n10. Payment instructions/payment status.\n11. Order success and order details.\n12. Track My Order.\n13. About Us.\n14. Contact Us.\n15. FAQ.\n16. My Account.\n17. My Orders and order history.\n18. Saved Addresses.\n19. Shipping Policy.\n20. Return and Refund Policy.\n21. Privacy Policy.\n22. Terms and Conditions.\n23. Custom 404 and useful error pages.\n\nPreserve the reference\'s bottom navigation and header placement. Use the same design language throughout.\n\nGuest checkout must work. Customer accounts may be offered but must not be mandatory to place an order.\n\n# 4. CATEGORIES AND INITIAL CATALOGUE\n\nCreate these categories:\n\n- Gift Hampers\n- Hair Accessories\n- Jewellery\n- Cosmetics & Beauty \342\200\224 Coming Soon\n- Festive Collection \342\200\224 Coming Soon\n\nCreate these initial catalogue entries as drafts until the business owner confirms actual contents, images and stock:\n\n- Pretty Charm Mini Hamper \342\200\224 proposed price \342\202\271299.\n- Pretty Charm Bestie Box \342\200\224 proposed price \342\202\271299.\n- Pretty Charm Premium Box \342\200\224 proposed price \342\202\271449.\n- Pretty Charm Celebration Box \342\200\224 proposed price \342\202\271449.\n\nDo not pretend these draft products are physically available. Let an authorized admin confirm the contents, images, quantities, descriptions and selling prices before publishing them.\n\nSupport real configured hair accessories, earrings, jewellery and related products.\n\nProduct fields:\n- Name and slug.\n- Description.\n- Category and tags.\n- Images.\n- SKU.\n- Variants.\n- Selling price.\n- Purchase cost.\n- Packaging cost.\n- Stock quantity.\n- Low-stock threshold.\n- Active/draft/coming-soon/out-of-stock/archived status.\n- Creation and update timestamps.\n\nThe Admin Panel must support product creation, editing, publishing, unpublishing, archiving and stock changes without code edits.\n\nNever allow a customer to purchase an unpublished, Coming Soon, archived or unavailable product.\n\n# 5. FRONTEND AND CUSTOMER EXPERIENCE\n\nImplement:\n- Search.\n- Category navigation.\n- Product sorting and filtering.\n- Product image gallery.\n- Variant selection.\n- Quantity controls.\n- Cart persistence.\n- Wishlist.\n- Recently viewed items.\n- Responsive loading and useful empty/error states.\n- Clear shipping charges.\n- Order confirmation.\n- Secure order tracking.\n\nUse local storage only for non-sensitive convenience data, such as the cart or wishlist. Never store passwords, payment secrets or sensitive customer records in local storage.\n\nUse accessible controls, proper labels, keyboard support and readable contrast.\n\nOptimize images and avoid unnecessary heavy packages. Do not compromise the reference UI for visual effects.\n\n# 6. DATABASE AND BACKEND\n\nUse a persistent database appropriate to the existing framework. If starting from scratch, choose a maintainable relational database and document the choice.\n\nCreate validated database models and migrations for:\n- Products.\n- Categories.\n- Product variants.\n- Inventory balances.\n- Inventory movements.\n- Suppliers.\n- Purchase orders and goods receipts.\n- Customers.\n- Addresses.\n- Shopping orders.\n- Order items.\n- Payment records.\n- Shipment records.\n- Shipment events.\n- Expenses.\n- Shipping rules and serviceable PIN codes.\n- Admin users and roles.\n- Audit logs.\n\nRequirements:\n- Server-side validation.\n- Database constraints.\n- Safe transaction handling.\n- Unique order IDs.\n- Correct currency arithmetic using integer paise or another reliable decimal representation.\n- Pagination for large lists.\n- Search and date filtering.\n- Consistent timestamps.\n- Safe migrations and backups.\n- Meaningful errors without exposing internal secrets.\n\nNever calculate final checkout totals solely in the browser. Recalculate product prices, stock, discounts and shipping on the server.\n\n# 7. CUSTOMER AUTHENTICATION AND ADMIN SECURITY\n\nImplement customer registration/login only if needed by the existing architecture; guest checkout remains available.\n\nAdmin access must be protected independently from customer access.\n\nImplement:\n- Secure password hashing.\n- Server-side authentication and authorization.\n- Role-based permissions.\n- Secure sessions and cookie configuration.\n- Login rate limiting.\n- Input validation.\n- CSRF protection where applicable.\n- Secure file upload validation.\n- Audit logging for sensitive admin actions.\n- Safe handling of expired sessions.\n- No default production admin password.\n- No publicly accessible customer exports.\n\nNever expose environment variables, database credentials, private API keys or service-role tokens in frontend bundles.\n\nCreate a documented, safe process to bootstrap the first administrator. Do not provide an open public endpoint that lets anyone become an admin.\n\n# 8. INVENTORY, PURCHASES AND SUPPLIERS\n\nBuild a functional inventory system.\n\nSupport:\n- Opening stock.\n- Supplier management.\n- Purchase records.\n- Goods received.\n- Stock increases and decreases.\n- Stock sold.\n- Returned stock.\n- Damaged stock.\n- Manual adjustments with reasons.\n- Reserved stock for valid orders.\n- Release of reservations after eligible cancellations.\n- Low-stock alerts.\n- Stock movement history.\n\nPrevent overselling using database transactions or appropriate atomic stock controls.\n\nUse a documented inventory valuation method, such as weighted average cost, and implement it consistently. Preserve historical product cost on each order item so later purchase-cost changes do not rewrite historical profitability.\n\nNever count unsold inventory as cost of goods sold.\n\n# 9. CHECKOUT AND ORDER MANAGEMENT\n\nCheckout fields:\n- Customer full name.\n- Mobile number.\n- Full delivery address.\n- House/flat/building.\n- Street/locality.\n- City.\n- State.\n- PIN code.\n- Optional delivery instructions.\n\nValidate fields on both client and server.\n\nSupport:\n- Guest checkout.\n- Unique order ID.\n- Order item snapshots.\n- Item price and quantity snapshots.\n- Shipping fee snapshot.\n- Order subtotal and final total.\n- Order creation timestamp.\n- Order status history.\n- Cancellation workflow.\n- Return/refund workflow with authorized status changes.\n\nSuggested internal statuses:\n- Pending Confirmation.\n- Confirmed.\n- Processing.\n- Packed.\n- Shipped.\n- Delivered.\n- Cancelled.\n- Return Requested.\n- Returned.\n- Refunded.\n\nKeep order status separate from payment status and courier shipment status.\n\nDo not create a real order until server-side validation succeeds. Do not reserve or deduct stock incorrectly if checkout fails.\n\n# 10. PAYMENT \342\200\224 SAFE STAGED IMPLEMENTATION\n\nInitially implement an honest payment configuration with these modes:\n\nA. Demo mode:\n- Clearly label it as a demo.\n- Never represent demo payments as real payments.\n- Never mark an order paid from a fake button.\n\nB. Manual payment workflow:\n- Allow an authorized admin to provide legitimate payment instructions after confirming the business can legally use the relevant account.\n- Store payment reference details only as necessary.\n- Keep the order awaiting verification until the owner checks the actual bank/payment account.\n- Record who verified payment and when.\n\nC. Payment gateway:\n- Prepare a provider adapter only if a supported provider is selected and valid credentials are supplied.\n- Verify payment status server-side using the provider\'s trusted API or signed webhook.\n- Handle duplicate webhook events safely.\n- Never trust a browser redirect, screenshot, UTR alone or frontend button as proof of payment.\n\nDo not bypass age restrictions, account terms, merchant onboarding or KYC. If payment eligibility is not confirmed, keep live payments disabled and explain the remaining setup.\n\n# 11. SHIPPING AND COURIER TRACKING\n\nShip from Gurugram to supported destinations in India.\n\nBuild a configurable shipping rules engine.\n\nInitial suggested customer-facing shipping fees:\n- Cart subtotal below \342\202\271499: \342\202\27159.\n- Cart subtotal \342\202\271499 to below \342\202\271999: \342\202\27139.\n- Cart subtotal \342\202\271999 or above: free shipping.\n\nThese are configurable selling rules, not guaranteed actual courier charges. Admin must be able to change them.\n\nDo not hardcode these fees as courier costs.\n\nSupport:\n- PIN-code validation.\n- Configurable serviceable PIN codes.\n- Courier name.\n- AWB/consignment number.\n- Official tracking URL.\n- Shipment pickup date.\n- Estimated delivery date when provided by a trusted carrier.\n- Shipment event history.\n- Last update timestamp.\n- Delivery and return events.\n\nCustomer tracking page:\n- Customer enters order ID and verifies the associated phone number.\n- Server returns only the minimum information necessary for that order.\n- Show order timeline, shipment status, courier, tracking number, tracking link and ETA if available.\n- Provide a clear unavailable state when tracking has not started.\n\nBuild a courier adapter suitable for Shiprocket or another supported provider.\n\nDo not enable automatic tracking until real credentials and valid configuration are provided. Verify webhook authenticity, map events to the correct shipment and prevent duplicate processing.\n\nNever fabricate GPS location, courier scans, pickup events, delivery dates or delivery confirmation.\n\nIf no trustworthy ETA is available, display \"Estimated delivery not available yet.\"\n\n# 12. WHATSAPP AND CUSTOMER NOTIFICATIONS\n\nProvide WhatsApp click-to-chat links for:\n- Order enquiry.\n- Order confirmation follow-up.\n- Shipment tracking.\n- Customer support.\n\nPrefill relevant information only after validating it, and avoid exposing sensitive customer data in public URLs.\n\nClick-to-chat does not send messages automatically; the customer or admin must press Send.\n\nFor automated notifications, support an official authorized integration only after valid credentials and permissions are configured.\n\nNever scrape WhatsApp or Instagram, request customer passwords or falsely display integrations as connected.\n\n# 13. ADMIN DASHBOARD\n\nCreate or complete these sections:\n\n1. Overview Dashboard.\n2. Products.\n3. Categories & Homepage.\n4. Inventory.\n5. Purchase & Suppliers.\n6. Orders.\n7. Customers.\n8. Sales & Revenue.\n9. Cost & Profit.\n10. Expenses.\n11. Shipping & PIN Codes.\n12. Reports & Excel Export.\n13. WhatsApp & Instagram Integrations.\n14. Store Settings.\n15. Admin Users & Audit Log.\n\nOverview dashboard:\n- Total products.\n- Active products.\n- Low-stock products.\n- Total orders.\n- Pending orders.\n- Paid revenue.\n- Refund totals.\n- Expense totals.\n- Estimated profit.\n- Recent orders.\n\nAll dashboard values must come from actual database queries. Clearly label demo or estimated data.\n\nUse charts and summary cards that match the existing design. Make the admin functional on desktop and mobile.\n\n# 14. SALES, PROFIT AND EXPENSES\n\nImplement date-filtered reporting for:\n- Sales.\n- Paid revenue.\n- Orders.\n- Average order value.\n- Product performance.\n- Inventory value.\n- Purchase costs.\n- Shipping costs.\n- Shipping fees collected.\n- Packaging costs.\n- Payment fees.\n- Refunds and returns.\n- Marketing and operating expenses.\n- Gross profit.\n- Contribution profit.\n- Estimated net profit.\n\nUse consistent documented formulas.\n\nDo not count unpaid orders as paid revenue. Handle cancellations, refunds, returns and shipping costs consistently. Avoid double counting.\n\nIf purchase costs, payment fees or other inputs are missing, mark profit as an estimate and identify the missing inputs.\n\nDo not treat sales revenue as profit.\n\n# 15. EXCEL AND CSV EXPORTS\n\nImplement secure downloads for:\n- Products.xlsx\n- Inventory.xlsx\n- PurchaseHistory.xlsx\n- Orders.xlsx\n- Customers.xlsx\n- SalesRevenue.xlsx\n- ProductProfitMargins.xlsx\n- OrderProfitability.xlsx\n- Expenses.xlsx\n- LowStock.xlsx\n\nAlso support CSV where useful.\n\nRequirements:\n- Export actual filtered database data.\n- Correct INR values and date formats.\n- Clear headers.\n- Appropriate totals.\n- Stable column ordering.\n- Useful filenames.\n- Proper spreadsheet escaping for untrusted cell content.\n- Authorization checks before every export.\n- Do not expose customer data to unauthorized users.\n\nUse a maintained spreadsheet library. Test generated files and handle large exports safely.\n\n# 16. CUSTOMER SUPPORT AND STORE CONTENT\n\nImplement About, Contact and FAQ pages using approved business details only.\n\nProvide editable store settings for:\n- Store name and logo.\n- Support contact details.\n- WhatsApp number.\n- Store address and shipping origin.\n- Shipping rules.\n- Return/refund policy text.\n- Social media links.\n- Announcement banner.\n\nDo not invent phone numbers, email addresses, business registrations or guarantees. Use clearly identified placeholders until the owner supplies the real details.\n\nPolicy pages must be editable and must not claim legal compliance that has not been verified.\n\n# 17. PWA, PERFORMANCE AND RELIABILITY\n\nImplement installable PWA support where compatible with the project.\n\nInclude:\n- Valid manifest.\n- Correct app icons.\n- Appropriate theme color.\n- Safe service-worker strategy.\n- Responsive images.\n- Useful loading states.\n- Error boundaries or equivalent error handling.\n- Accessible controls.\n- SEO metadata.\n- Correct canonical URLs when a real domain is configured.\n\nDo not cache private account, payment or checkout responses in a public cache.\n\n# 18. TESTING \342\200\224 REQUIRED BEFORE COMPLETION\n\nWrite and run appropriate tests for:\n- Product CRUD.\n- Product publication rules.\n- Stock availability.\n- Cart totals.\n- Shipping calculations.\n- Server-side price validation.\n- Guest checkout.\n- Order creation.\n- Duplicate order submission.\n- Payment verification.\n- Authorization failures.\n- Inventory concurrency.\n- Cancellation and stock release.\n- Shipment tracking.\n- Invalid order lookup.\n- Incorrect phone verification.\n- Duplicate courier webhooks.\n- Missing ETA.\n- Refund calculations.\n- Profit reporting.\n- Excel/CSV export permissions.\n\nAlso test:\n- All major screens.\n- All primary buttons and links.\n- Empty states.\n- API error handling.\n- Mobile layouts at 360, 375, 390 and 430 pixels.\n- Keyboard navigation where applicable.\n\nRun the project\'s lint, type-check, test suite and production build. Fix failures caused by your implementation.\n\nDo not claim a test passed unless it was actually run and passed.\n\n# 19. DOCUMENTATION AND DEPLOYMENT\n\nCreate or update:\n- README.md.\n- .env.example containing placeholder values only.\n- Database schema and migration instructions.\n- Windows setup instructions.\n- Development start commands.\n- Production build instructions.\n- Deployment instructions.\n- Backup and restore instructions.\n- Admin bootstrap instructions.\n- Payment and courier setup documentation.\n- Troubleshooting guide.\n\nAdd and verify .gitignore entries for environment files, credentials, build output, dependency folders, local databases and other sensitive files as appropriate.\n\nBefore any Git push:\n- Check for secrets and private customer data.\n- Never commit a real .env file or credentials.\n- Do not rewrite Git history or force-push without explicit permission.\n- Do not automatically publish private code publicly.\n\nIf GitHub is connected and authorization is available, help prepare a safe commit. Otherwise provide the exact commands for the owner to run.\n\nDeploy only when the required hosting account, environment variables, database and external credentials are configured. Do not claim a successful deployment without a verified result.\n\n# 20. EXECUTION STRATEGY \342\200\224 DO NOT STOP AFTER PLANNING\n\nExecute the following phases in sequence:\n\nPhase 1: Inspect workspace and audit existing implementation.\nPhase 2: Create or repair architecture, environment configuration and database.\nPhase 3: Implement the approved UI and customer storefront.\nPhase 4: Implement catalogue, product administration and inventory.\nPhase 5: Implement checkout, order processing and customer order history.\nPhase 6: Implement payment configuration and verification safeguards.\nPhase 7: Implement shipping, tracking and WhatsApp click-to-chat.\nPhase 8: Implement dashboard, expenses, profit calculations and reports.\nPhase 9: Implement Excel/CSV exports and integrations configuration.\nPhase 10: Implement security hardening, PWA and performance improvements.\nPhase 11: Run tests, inspect errors, fix regressions and compare the UI with the reference.\nPhase 12: Document setup, environment variables, deployment and remaining external setup.\n\nAfter each phase:\n- Summarize what was completed.\n- Run the relevant checks.\n- Fix implementation errors before moving on.\n- Maintain a list of incomplete items.\n- Continue automatically to the next phase unless a genuine blocker requires owner input.\n\nIf an external credential is missing, complete the code path with secure configuration placeholders and continue with unrelated work. Never invent credentials or claim a live integration works.\n\nIf the environment cannot execute a required action, explain the limitation and give the exact next step.\n\n# 21. FINAL ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\n\nThe project is complete only when:\n- The existing approved UI is preserved.\n- The storefront uses persistent product data.\n- Admin authentication and authorization work.\n- Product and inventory management work.\n- Checkout creates validated orders.\n- Payment states cannot be forged from the frontend.\n- Order tracking uses genuine shipment data.\n- Reports use real database data and documented calculations.\n- Excel/CSV exports work and are access-controlled.\n- The mobile interface is tested.\n- Critical automated checks pass.\n- The production build succeeds.\n- Setup and deployment instructions are documented.\n- Remaining external requirements are clearly listed.\n\nBegin by inspecting the current project workspace and the uploaded Pretty Charm UI reference. Then start implementing immediately. Do not return only a plan, tutorial, sample code or mockup. Build the working application step by step and report actual results."
