  view_file {
    absolute_path_uri: "file:///skills/system_skills/computer_use/SKILL.md"
    end_line: 306
    content: "---\nname: computer-use\ndescription: >-\n  Browser automation, web browsing, interactive UI testing, DOM/console\n  inspection, and visual screenshot or video capture for any website or URL\n  using Playwright CLI (`playwright-cli`). Trigger this skill whenever the user\n  asks to open, browse, inspect, scrape, interact with, test, debug, screenshot,\n  or record any external website, reference URL, or the locally running web\n  application (`http://localhost:3000`), or when you need to verify rendered UI\n  behavior, animations, DOM state, or browser console/network errors.\n---\n\n# Computer Use & Browser Automation (`playwright-cli`)\n\nThis skill equips you to launch a headless Chromium browser inside the applet\ncontainer using `@playwright/cli` (`playwright-cli`) to open and interact with\n**any website or URL**\342\200\224including public websites (e.g., for design inspiration,\nresearch, or live reference inspection) as well as the locally running applet\n(`http://localhost:3000`). You can navigate pages, click and fill elements,\ninspect accessibility snapshots, console logs, and network requests, and capture\n`.png` screenshots or `.webm` screen recordings.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## Command Quick Reference\n\nBoth `playwright-cli` and headless Chromium (`/ms-playwright`) are pre-installed\nin the container image, and `.playwright-cli/` is ignored by default. You can\nrun these commands directly without running the fallback setup in Section 1:\n\n```bash\n# Session & navigation\nplaywright-cli open --browser=chromium http://localhost:3000\nplaywright-cli goto http://localhost:3000/settings\nplaywright-cli reload\nplaywright-cli resize 1440 900\nplaywright-cli close\nplaywright-cli kill-all\n\n# Snapshots & DOM inspection (returns accessibility tree with [ref=eN] handles)\nplaywright-cli snapshot\nplaywright-cli snapshot --depth=4\nplaywright-cli find \"Add to cart\"\nplaywright-cli find --regex \"/sign (in|up)/i\"\nplaywright-cli eval \"document.title\"\nplaywright-cli eval \"el => el.textContent\" e5\n\n# Interactions (target by snapshot ref eN, CSS selector, or role locator)\nplaywright-cli click e3\nplaywright-cli dblclick e7\nplaywright-cli fill e5 \"user@example.com\"\nplaywright-cli fill e5 \"search query\" --submit\nplaywright-cli type \"raw keyboard text\"\nplaywright-cli press Enter\nplaywright-cli select e9 \"option-value\"\nplaywright-cli check e12\nplaywright-cli uncheck e12\nplaywright-cli hover e4\nplaywright-cli dialog-accept\n\n# Captures & DevTools\nplaywright-cli screenshot --filename=.playwright-cli/r1-desktop.png\nplaywright-cli video-start .playwright-cli/clip.webm\nplaywright-cli video-stop\nplaywright-cli console\nplaywright-cli requests\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## On-Demand Fallback Setup & Reference Guides\n\nDo **not** run Section 1 or Section 2 before writing application code. Run the\nSection 1 setup block only if `playwright-cli` is missing from `PATH` or if you\nneed to copy `.playwright-cli/skill/` into the workspace to read one of the\nspecialized reference guides in Section 2.\n\n### 1. One-Shot Setup (On-Demand Only)\n\n```bash\n# 1. Use the preinstalled global playwright-cli; install it globally if missing\nif ! command -v playwright-cli >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n  BUN_INSTALL_BIN=/usr/local/bin bun add -g @playwright/cli@latest \\\n    2>/dev/null || npm install -g @playwright/cli@latest\nfi\n\n# 2. Configure gitignore and working directory\nif ! grep -qxF \'.playwright-cli/\' .gitignore 2>/dev/null; then\n  echo \'.playwright-cli/\' >> .gitignore\nfi\nmkdir -p .playwright-cli\n\n# 3. Copy the bundled, version-matched playwright-cli skill into the project\nrm -rf .playwright-cli/skill\nif [ -d /ms-playwright/skill ]; then\n  cp -r /ms-playwright/skill .playwright-cli/skill\nelse\n  PW_PKG=\"$(dirname \"$(readlink -f \"$(command -v playwright-cli)\")\")\"\n  cp -r \"$PW_PKG/skills/playwright-cli\" .playwright-cli/skill\nfi\n\n# 4. Download Playwright\'s Chromium binary if not already in cache\nCACHE_DIR=\"${PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH:-$HOME/.cache/ms-playwright}\"\nif ! ls -d \"$CACHE_DIR\"/chromium* >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n  playwright-cli install-browser chromium --only-shell\nfi\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2. Bundled Playwright CLI References (On-Demand Only)\n\nIf your task requires one of the specialized workflows below, run step 3 of\nSection 1 first so `.playwright-cli/skill/` exists in the workspace, then read\nthe corresponding guide under `/.playwright-cli/skill/references/`:\n\n-   `element-attributes.md` \342\200\224 Inspecting DOM attributes, classes, and computed\n    styles not visible in snapshots.\n-   `request-mocking.md` \342\200\224 Mocking and intercepting network traffic (`route` /\n    `unroute`).\n-   `running-code.md` \342\200\224 Running custom multi-step Playwright scripts via\n    `run-code`.\n-   `session-management.md` \342\200\224 Managing isolated or persistent browser sessions\n    (`-s=`, `list`, `close-all`, `kill-all`).\n-   `storage-state.md` \342\200\224 Saving and loading cookies, `localStorage`,\n    `sessionStorage`, and auth state.\n-   `tracing.md` \342\200\224 Capturing execution traces (`tracing-start` /\n    `tracing-stop`).\n-   `video-recording.md` \342\200\224 Recording browser sessions, chapters, and overlays\n    (`video-start` / `video-stop` and `page.screencast`).\n-   `playwright-tests.md` & `test-generation.md` \342\200\224 Running, debugging,\n    generating, and healing Playwright tests.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## AI Studio Container Rules\n\nWhen using `playwright-cli`, always apply these container-specific rules:\n\n1.  **Invoke the global `playwright-cli` binary directly (never `bunx` or\n    `npx`)**: `playwright-cli` is pre-installed on the `PATH`, so run commands\n    as `playwright-cli <command>`. If the shell reports `playwright-cli: command\n    not found`, run the Section 1 setup block immediately. **Never** run `bunx\n    playwright-cli` or `npx playwright-cli` (the unscoped `playwright-cli` npm\n    package is a different, unrelated package), **never** add `@playwright/cli`\n    to the app\'s `package.json`, and **never** write standalone Node scripts\n    that import `playwright` (`chromium.launch()`).\n2.  **Always use headless Chromium (`--browser=chromium` or default `open`),\n    never `--browser=chrome` or `--headed`**: Only headless Chromium\n    (`/ms-playwright/chrome-headless-shell-linux64/chrome-headless-shell`) is\n    installed in the container. Start sessions with `playwright-cli open <url>`\n    (or `playwright-cli open --browser=chromium <url>`, both of which use the\n    container\'s preconfigured `~/.playwright/cli.config.json`). **Never** pass\n    `--browser=chrome`, `--browser=firefox`, `--browser=webkit`, or\n    `--browser=msedge` (even though they appear in the bundled `SKILL.md`\n    examples\342\200\224system Google Chrome at `/opt/google/chrome/chrome` is not\n    installed), and **never** use `--headed` or GUI-only commands like `show\n    --annotate`.\n3.  **Pass relative paths to CLI flags, and read files relative to root `/`**:\n    -   In shell commands, always pass paths **relative to the project root**\n        (no leading `/`), inside `.playwright-cli/` (e.g.,\n        `--filename=.playwright-cli/r1-desktop.png`,\n        `.playwright-cli/clip.webm`, `.playwright-cli/dom.yaml`). Writing to\n        `/...` in the shell writes to the OS root outside the project, and\n        writing to `.` pollutes the user\'s application files.\n    -   When reading files (screenshots, videos, or the copied skill docs), the\n        project directory itself is rooted at `/`. Always prepend `/` to the\n        project-relative path (e.g., read `/.playwright-cli/r1-desktop.png` or\n        `/.playwright-cli/skill/SKILL.md`). **Never** prepend the shell\'s `pwd`\n        or `realpath` output when reading files.\n4.  **Store user-requested deliverables in\n    `.aistudio/artifacts/computer_use/`**: Because `.playwright-cli/` is\n    gitignored, files inside it are hidden from the user\'s file explorer. Only\n    when the user explicitly asks you to save a screenshot or recording for them\n    to view/download, pass `.aistudio/artifacts/computer_use/<name>` to the CLI\n    (`mkdir -p .aistudio/artifacts/computer_use`) and read it at\n    `/.aistudio/artifacts/computer_use/<name>`. For your own validation, always\n    keep files in `.playwright-cli/`.\n5.  **Choose the right inspection modality (`snapshot`, `screenshot`, `video`)\n    and always read the captured file**: Saving a `.png` or `.webm` to disk does\n    not validate anything until you read the file to inspect it:\n\n    -   **DOM & text state (`snapshot`)**: Use `playwright-cli snapshot` (or\n        `find`) for fast, low-token inspection of text content, DOM structure,\n        and `eN` element refs.\n    -   **Static visual layout (`screenshot` + read, unique filenames)**: When\n        verifying visual design, layout, styling, responsiveness (e.g. after\n        `resize`), or static canvas/3D rendering, capture a `.png` screenshot\n        with a **unique filename per capture** (e.g., `playwright-cli screenshot\n        --filename=.playwright-cli/r1-desktop.png`) and read\n        `/.playwright-cli/r1-desktop.png`. **Never** overwrite a screenshot path\n        that was already read, and keep screenshot reads bounded (at most 4 per\n        turn, using `snapshot` for intermediate DOM checks).\n    -   **Animations, scrolling, & time-based behavior (`video-start` /\n        `video-stop` + read)**: When verifying animations, transitions, scroll\n        effects, interactive gestures, physics/collisions, canvas/3D loops, or\n        UI that updates over time, record a `.webm` video inside\n        `.playwright-cli/` and read `/.playwright-cli/clip.webm` to watch it:\n\n        ```bash\n        playwright-cli video-start .playwright-cli/clip.webm\n        # Perform interactions, scroll (mousewheel), or sleep while UI updates\n        playwright-cli mousewheel 0 500\n        sleep 2\n        playwright-cli video-stop\n        ```\n\n        For scripted pauses or visual callout overlays during recording, see\n        `references/video-recording.md` (listed in Section 2).\n\n6.  **Run commands sequentially, wait when backgrounded, and always trap `close`\n    on exit**:\n\n    -   Commands targeting the same browser session (the default session or a\n        named `-s=<name>` session) share a live browser instance. **Never**\n        start a follow-up command (`screenshot`, `snapshot`, `click`) while a\n        previous command on that session (`open`, `goto`, `video-stop`) is still\n        running in the background.\n    -   If a command is sent to the background, **stop calling tools and wait**\n        for the completion notification\342\200\224never poll background task status in a\n        loop, and **never** attempt to read a screenshot or snapshot file before\n        the background command has finished writing it.\n    -   **Always close the browser (`playwright-cli close`)** once visual\n        verification is complete so background Chromium processes do not keep\n        rendering WebGL/canvas loops and exhaust container CPU/memory while you\n        edit code. When chaining multiple steps in a single shell command,\n        **always register `trap \'playwright-cli close\' EXIT` at the top** (never\n        gate `close` behind `&&`, which skips closing if an earlier step fails\n        or times out):\n\n        ```bash\n        trap \'playwright-cli close\' EXIT\n        mkdir -p .playwright-cli && \\\n          playwright-cli open --browser=chromium http://localhost:3000 && \\\n          playwright-cli resize 1440 900 && \\\n          playwright-cli screenshot --filename=.playwright-cli/r1-desktop.png\n        ```\n\n7.  **Restart the dev server after code edits (`DISABLE_HMR=true`)**: Hot Module\n    Replacement is disabled in the container. After editing application code,\n    restart the dev server before running `playwright-cli reload` or re-opening\n    `http://localhost:3000` so you do not inspect stale code.\n\n8.  **Headless WebGL/Canvas performance & `screenshot` timeout recovery**:\n\n    -   Headless Chromium here renders WebGL on the CPU (SwiftShader; no GPU).\n        If `open` or `screenshot` stalls on a WebGL / Three.js app, the app is\n        rendering back to back: switch it to the software-WebGL path in the\n        `threejs` skill (on-demand rendering, `afterDraw()` game loop) instead\n        of retrying the capture.\n    -   The container\'s `~/.playwright/cli.config.json` configures a 20,000ms\n        action timeout (`\"timeouts\": { \"action\": 20000 }`) for `playwright-cli\n        screenshot` (`screenshot` **does not accept a `--timeout` flag**).\n        Prefer `playwright-cli screenshot --filename=.playwright-cli/view.png`\n        directly. If `screenshot` still times out on a heavy WebGL/Three.js\n        canvas, use `run-code` with a single positional `(page)` argument (not\n        `{ page }` and not a bare `await` statement):\n\n        ```bash\n        playwright-cli run-code \'async (page) => {\n          await page.screenshot({\n            path: \".playwright-cli/r1-desktop.png\",\n            timeout: 20000,\n          });\n        }\'\n        ```\n\n    -   If `open` or `screenshot` hangs or fails, check `playwright-cli console`\n        for runtime errors, and run `playwright-cli kill-all` before retrying\n        `open` so orphaned Chromium processes do not exhaust container memory.\n\n9.  **Drive canvas/WebGL content with coordinate and keyboard input, not locator\n    clicks**:\n\n    -   Locator clicks (`playwright-cli click <ref>` or `getByRole(...).click()`\n        inside `run-code`) wait until the target is actionable and pending\n        navigations settle. On a busy, software-rendered `requestAnimationFrame`\n        loop these waits can stall until they time out (30s inside `run-code`).\n        Canvas internals also have no snapshot refs.\n    -   For gameplay and other input inside a `<canvas>`, use the raw input\n        commands. They dispatch events immediately without actionability checks:\n        `mousemove <x> <y>`, `mousedown [button]`, `mouseup [button]`,\n        `mousewheel <dx> <dy>`, `press <key>`, `keydown <key>`, and `keyup\n        <key>`.\n    -   Coordinates are viewport CSS pixels. Read the canvas bounds first:\n\n        ```bash\n        playwright-cli eval \"() => JSON.stringify(\n          document.querySelector(\'canvas\').getBoundingClientRect())\"\n        ```\n\n    -   To check held input (movement, aiming, charging), hold the key or\n        button, capture while it is held, then release:\n\n        ```bash\n        trap \'playwright-cli close\' EXIT\n        playwright-cli open http://localhost:3000 && \\\n          playwright-cli mousemove 640 400 && \\\n          playwright-cli mousedown && playwright-cli mouseup && \\\n          playwright-cli keydown ArrowRight && sleep 1 && \\\n          playwright-cli screenshot --filename=.playwright-cli/hold.png\n        playwright-cli keyup ArrowRight\n        ```\n\n    -   Keep ref-based `click` for regular DOM controls (menus, buttons, and\n        forms, including HTML overlays on top of a canvas).\n"
    num_lines: 307
    num_bytes: 15104
    is_skill_file: true
    skill_metadata {
      name: "computer-use"
      description: "Browser automation, web browsing, interactive UI testing, DOM/console inspection, and visual screenshot or video capture for any website or URL using Playwright CLI (`playwright-cli`). Trigger this skill whenever the user asks to open, browse, inspect, scrape, interact with, test, debug, screenshot, or record any external website, reference URL, or the locally running web application (`http://localhost:3000`), or when you need to verify rendered UI behavior, animations, DOM state, or browser console/network errors."
    }
