    thinking: "**Analyzing Pin Code Data**\n\nI\'m now focusing on Test #6 within `src/shared/domainLogic.test.ts`, specifically examining the `SAMPLE_PINS` data structure. I\'ve noted the entry for pincode \'400001\', which explicitly indicates its unserviceable status.\n\n\n**Investigating Logic Implementation**\n\nI\'m now diving into the implementation of `checkPinCodeServiceability`, specifically lines 268-297 in `src/shared/domainLogic.ts`. It seems like the core logic revolves around how it handles missing matches in `configuredPins`. I want to confirm that valid, unlisted Indian PIN codes are treated as serviceable by default. If this holds true, it clarifies why Test #6, despite its assertion about \'400001\', can still pass while real PIN codes remain valid.\n\n\n**Refining Serviceability Logic**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the conditional logic within the `checkPinCodeServiceability` function. I\'m proposing a refined approach. If no matching PIN code is found (the `!match` case), it will return as serviceable. This is because all valid PINs are serviceable by default, but any explicit unserviceable matches will be captured. This approach will ensure format validation and explicit denials for unserviceable PIN codes.\n\n\n"
